import
	../../html

intro-type-parameters html[]()
	- section "Type parameters for functions",
		- p quote
			Functions can declare type parameters.
			Below, {fn-swap} can work on a pair of any type.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a (nat, nat) = 1, 2
				log swap a
				b (string, string) = "world", "hello"
				log swap b

			swap[t] (t, t)(a (t, t))
				a.b, a.a

		- p quote
			In {fn-swap}{sp}{var-a} , {nat} is substituted for {t}.
			{br}
			In {fn-swap}{sp}{var-b} , {string} is substituted for {t}.

		- p "A type parameter must be consistent, so {fn-swap} would not work on a {type "(nat, string)"}."

		- p quote
			Within the body of {fn-swap}, {t} is treated as "any type";
			it can't be passed to another function unless that function also takes it as a type parameter.
			(The tuple getters {fn-a} and {fn-b} do so.)
			{br}
			In the next section, {intro-link specs}, we'll see how to add more useful operations on type parameters.

		- aside-small "Type parameters are almost always named {t} (then {u}, etc.), but they can have any name."

	- section "Explicit type arguments",
		- p "An explicit type argument can be specified using {keyword "@"}."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a (nat, nat) = 1, 2
				log swap@nat a
				b (string, string) = "world", "hello"
				log swap@string b

			swap[t] (t, t)(a (t, t))
				a.b, a.a

		- p quote
			If there are multiple type arguments, put them in parentheses.

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a (nat, string) = 1, "foo"
				log swap@(nat, string) a

			swap[t, u] (u, t)(a (t, u))
				a.b, a.a

		- p quote
			You should almost never use explicit type arguments,
			but they can help diagnose errors if Keen doesn't know what function you're trying to call.
			Then remove the explicit type arguments once it's working.

	- section "Type parameters for types",
		- p "A type can itself have type parameters."

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a nat ranker = best: 7, worst: 13
				log worst invert a

			ranker[t] record
				best t
				worst t
			invert[t] t ranker(a t ranker)
				a.worst, a.best

		- p quote
			Types with type parameters use a suffix syntax:
			{type "t ranker"} is a {ranker} of {t}, just like {type "t[]"} is an {array} of {t}.
			{br}
			The suffixes can stack, so you can have a {type "t ranker[]"} (array of {ranker}s) or
			a {type "t[] ranker"} (ranker arrays).
		- p quote
			Type annotations such types may require parentheses, as in
			{fn-foo}{colon-colon}{lparen}{nat}{sp}{ranker}{rparen} .
		- p quote
			If there are multiple type parameters, write them like a tuple, as in
			{lparen}{nat}{comma}{string}{rparen}{sp}{type "pair"} .

		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				a (nat, string) pair = 1, "foo"
				log a.a, a.b

			pair[t, u] record
				a t
				b u

-ranker html-node()
	type "ranker"
-fn-swap html-node()
	function "swap"
